<div class="section-eyebrow">The framing problem</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Almost everything we hear about Hormuz is about oil.</h2>

<p class="prose">Six months into the closure, the oil numbers are well covered: transit down to a few percentage points of normal, war risk premiums at multiples of the pre-crisis rate, Brent up more than twenty percent. But the Middle East Gulf is also a major global source of urea and sulphur which are both byproducts of the region's oil and gas refining, and that trade has been disrupted alongside oil since the closure began on 28 February. India, Sri Lanka, and Thailand are the most exposed importers. This also has knock-on food security implications: urea is fertilizer, and a fertilizer supply shock reaches a farmer's input costs the next planting season.</p>

